The C program is small: it [38;5;10matoi[0ms [38;5;10margv[1][0m and [38;5;10margv[2][0m, stores them in [38;5;10mstruct test { int a; int b; }[0m, recovers the struct from each member pointer via [38;5;10mcontainer_of[0m, and prints [38;5;10ma + b[0m with [38;5;10m%d\n[0m. The [38;5;10mcontainer_of[0m round-trip is a no-op semantically, so the observable behavior is the sum — but I kept the struct, the offset arithmetic, and both helper functions in place so the structure mirrors the original.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The parts that needed care to avoid accidentally "fixing" the C:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **glibc [38;5;10matoi[0m semantics.** It is [38;5;10m(int) strtol(s, NULL, 10)[0m, so digits accumulate into a [38;5;10mlong[0m that saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m, and the result is then [3mtruncated[23m to [38;5;10mint[0m — not clamped. [38;5;10m"99999999999"[0m gives [38;5;10m1215752191[0m, [38;5;10m"9223372036854775808"[0m gives [38;5;10m-1[0m, and [38;5;10m"-99999999999999999999"[0m gives [38;5;10m0[0m. I reimplemented this directly (whitespace skip, optional sign, saturating i64 accumulate, [38;5;10mas i32[0m).[0m[0m
- **Signed overflow.** [38;5;10m2147483647 + 1[0m prints [38;5;10m-2147483648[0m under gcc, so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m.[0m[0m
- **Missing arguments.** With fewer than two args, [38;5;10matoi(NULL)[0m segfaults in glibc. I reproduced this (SIGSEGV, exit 139, no output) with a volatile read from a null pointer, evaluated in the same order as the C — [38;5;10margv[1][0m before [38;5;10margv[2][0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Argument bytes are read via [38;5;10margs_os[0m/[38;5;10mOsStrExt[0m rather than [38;5;10margs()[0m, so non-UTF-8 arguments behave like C rather than panicking. Only two small [38;5;10munsafe[0m blocks exist: the [38;5;10mcontainer_of[0m offset arithmetic (in-bounds, matching the macro) and the deliberate null read; everything else is safe.[0m[0m
